\f0\fs24 \cf2 In the Sala della Stufa Valtellinese, there are three small details that often go unnoticed, yet they tell a grand story of ingenuity and beauty. Take a close look at the wooden band above the boiserie: there, three fascinating instruments are hidden. On the left wall, there\'92s a barometer, above the fireplace a Ptolemaic planetarium, and above the piano a clock. Three dials, three different worlds. All made in 1884 by the refined artisan Pasquale Pisoni. Yet, the true protagonist is the barometer: the original drawing of it is still preserved, created in black ink by the Bagatti Valsecchi brothers. A meticulous design, kept in the museum's archives, revealing all the care and passion with which they imagined every detail of the house.\
The result? A barometer painted in blue, decorated with golden threads, combining scientific precision with artistic splendor. An object that measures the air, yes, but also tells us a dream made of art, science, and vision.\
